ralph/progress.txt:154:- Security-verification package-confinement pattern, backup-encryption instance (BE-0362 pattern, internal/release/backup_encryption_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/backup/encryption_isolation_test.go + SECURITY.md "## Backup Encryption" section + SECURITY.md Required Verification Gates table row + internal/controlplane/backup/AGENTS.md "Backup encryption boundary (BE-0362)" subsection; EIGHTH worked instance of the BE-0344 two-test template and FIRST whole-package-confinement instance — confirms the template scales from "this single function MUST honour rule X" (BE-0346), "this directory MUST NOT import surface Y" (BE-0357 admin endpoints, BE-0349 SSRF), and "this cross-package secret MUST flow through one seam" (BE-0361 Dokploy token) to "this leaf package MUST NOT carry an entire data plane". Eight load-bearing rules: (1) IMPORT-WHITELIST-AS-FORBIDDEN-LIST — the backup package's only legitimate I/O is os.ReadFile on the operator-supplied status path, so the gate enumerates the symmetric/asymmetric cipher set (crypto/aes, crypto/cipher, crypto/des, crypto/rc4, crypto/rsa, crypto/ecdsa, crypto/ed25519, crypto/tls), the archive/compression set (archive/zip, archive/tar, compress/gzip, compress/zlib, compress/flate, compress/bzip2), the database driver set (database/sql, github.com/jackc/pgx/v5, github.com/jackc/pgx/v5/pgxpool), and the process-boundary set (os/exec, net/http) as a closed forbidden map. The set is intentionally aggressive because the boundary contract is "no data plane", not "this specific library is bad"; a future contributor who needs one of these libraries must surface the design change in review, not slip it in. (2) INTERFACE-METHOD-COUNT-AND-NAMES — the read-only Reporter interface is pinned by name AND by its single method's name (Status). The matcher locates the InterfaceType named Reporter, rejects every field in iface.Methods.List whose len(Names)==0 (embedded interfaces would let any method through, e.g. embed io.Writer would silently expand the surface), and rejects every named method whose Name != "Status". A missing interface fires a separate "missing required interface" diagnostic — a future refactor that renamed Reporter to BackupSurface would silently disable the gate otherwise. (3) CALL-EXPR-SELECTOR write-seam scan — the package MUST NOT call package-qualified write/exec functions. The matcher walks every CallExpr, asserts call.Fun is a SelectorExpr whose X is an ast.Ident (package alias) and joins pkg.Name + "." + sel.Sel.Name to compare against forbiddenBackupWriteSeams (os.Create, os.CreateTemp, os.WriteFile, os.OpenFile, os.Mkdir, os.MkdirAll, os.Rename, os.Remove, os.RemoveAll, os.Symlink, os.Link, os.Truncate, os.Chmod, os.Chown, exec.Command, exec.CommandContext). os.ReadFile / os.Stat / os.Open are deliberately excluded — they are the legitimate read-only seams. (4) MARKER-PATTERN runtime evidence with FOUR encryption-shaped fixtures — a parameterised subtest installs the BE0362KMSMARKERXYZ prefix inside four payload shapes (kms-key-id with arn:aws:kms:, aes-wrap-blob with base64-shaped repeated bytes, restore-failed-message with multi-line bucket URL, pem-private-key with BEGIN/END markers) and asserts the marker never appears in (a) err.Error(), (b) every errors.Unwrap layer (TestFileReporterEncryptionMarkerSurvivesErrorWrapping walks the wrap chain — a regression that wrapped raw content inside a deeper %w would be caught), or (c) json.Marshal(Status) (defensive: the struct has no content field today, but a future Detail field would surface the operator's accidentally-seeded payload through every downstream projection). (5) FIXTURE-SHAPE diversity for redaction robustness — every fixture exercises a different class of regression a future "improvement" to the parse-error message might introduce; the marker is what makes the leak detector load-bearing across all four shapes, because asserting on the fixture's full content would false-positive whenever the content happens to share a substring with a stable error phrase (e.g. a fixture containing "is malformed" would trip the existing "backup: status file is malformed" message). (6) WRAP-ROBUST DOC SUBSTRINGS — when pinning Markdown content from a wrapped document (SECURITY.md and the runbook), choose substrings short enough to survive line wrapping. The first iteration used "Plaintext WAL must never" and "no read access" — both spanned line wraps in the source files ("Plaintext WAL must\n   never" and "**no** read access") and the test fired false positives. Fix: use "Plaintext WAL" and "read access" — same semantic, robust to Markdown wrap and to **bold** runs. The closed substring list still names every load-bearing fact (KMS-managed key for at-rest, read access for the no-access invariant, Plaintext WAL for the WAL ban, Auditability for restore audit events) without being brittle to formatting. (7) DUAL-DOC PIN — the gate pins TWO operator-facing documents: SECURITY.md (the public posture) AND docs/operations/backup-restore.md (the operator runbook). Each gets its own test function with its own heading constant and closed substring set. Pinning both prevents a future "tidy" pass from collapsing one without the other — the operator-facing runbook is part of the public contract because operators rely on it to discover the KMS rotation cadence, the no-read-access invariant, and the audit-event posture. (8) SELF-CHECK with KNOWN-GOOD AND KNOWN-BAD pairs for EVERY matcher — TestBackupEncryptionStaticAnalyzerDetectsRegressions feeds three pairs (imports, reporter-interface, write-seams) through every matcher. Each known-bad fixture must produce ≥N hits (N matches the synthetic regression count); each known-good fixture must produce zero false positives. The reporter-interface self-check has a THIRD synthetic — a renamed interface (Surface) — that proves the matcher fires a "missing required interface" diagnostic when Reporter is removed, not just when its method set widens. A self-check that only feeds known-bad through the matcher cannot catch over-tightening (a matcher that fires on legitimate code would still satisfy the bad-fixture assertions); a self-check that only feeds known-good cannot catch under-tightening (a matcher that fires on nothing would still satisfy the good-fixture assertions). Both pairs are load-bearing. Forward-applicable to ANY future security-verification story whose invariant is "this leaf package MUST NOT carry a data plane for cross-cutting concern X" — credential vaulting (the secrets package must not encrypt under a process-resident key), audit shipping (the audit package must not network out to a SIEM), rate-limit storage (the ratelimit package must not persist a token-bucket distributed store), backup restore orchestration (a future restore package's port shape), and KMS proxying (any future package that mediates KMS calls).
ralph/progress.txt:217:- Endpoint-pinned twin of an action-level engine-only policy matrix (BE-0288 pattern, internal/controlplane/policy/admin_dokploy_reconcile_endpoint_policy_test.go, sibling of BE-0285's admin_reconcile_policy_test.go): when two PRD stories cover two distinct WIRE ENDPOINTS that share the SAME action constant (admin.reconcile here — GET /v1/admin/dokploy/drift + POST /v1/admin/dokploy/reconcile), each story gets its own engine-only matrix file, structurally parallel but state-independent. Three load-bearing rules: (1) every canonical id (project / sibling / service-account principal) is renamed with the endpoint-distinguishing suffix so a future shared helper cannot accidentally couple the two matrices — BE-0288 uses canonicalAdminReconcileEndpointProjectID / canonicalAdminReconcileEndpointSiblingID and ServiceAccount IDs suffixed `_reconcile_endpoint`, deliberately distinct from BE-0285's `_reconcile` suffix; (2) docstrings frame the engine pin around the SPECIFIC endpoint's failure mode — BE-0285's file frames the action as "the contract admin.reconcile must satisfy regardless of which endpoint surfaces it" (action-level), BE-0288's file frames it as "the engine contract specifically for the mutating reconcile verb where an asymmetric regression that left listing intact but downgraded mutation capability cannot ship without flipping one of these expectations" (endpoint-level); (3) the test surface is structurally identical seven-function shape (Roles / Revoked&Expired / WrongOrg / Project / Env / Service / OrgGrant / ForeignHomeOrg) and verdict matrix is byte-identical to the action-level twin — the point of the duplication is endpoint-specific regression coverage, not behavioural difference at the engine seam. Applicable to any future PRD story pair where the wire endpoint differs but the policy action is shared (currently unique to admin.reconcile in BE-0285+0288; future similar pairs would clone this template). NOT applicable when two endpoints have distinct action constants — those follow the BE-0279/0282 sibling-action template instead, where the differentiator is the action's capability not the endpoint's role.
